[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns “green disappears in the forest, brown disappears on the field” into **icons that stay readable on any satellite / aerial image**.  
All of the tricks come from the *MapLibre-Cartography* skill – halo/outline, SDF sprites, contact-shadows, colour choice, layer ordering and a few small-scale performance tips.

---

## 1. Choose the right *visual language* for imagery

| Why it matters | What to use on imagery |
|----------------|------------------------|
| **Background colour is unpredictable** (bright crops, dark forest, water, roofs) | **White or very light-saturated fill** for the *icon body* (e.g. `#ffffff` or `#f5f5f5`). |
| **Chroma is the weakest dimension** in most photos (they are low-saturation) | **Add a dark, semi-transparent halo/outline** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`). This creates contrast on both bright and dark surfaces. |
| **You still need a semantic colour cue** (e.g. “park = green”, “historic = amber”) | Keep the *semantic hue* **inside the halo** – either as a thin inner ring or as a coloured “badge” that sits inside the white body. The outer halo does the legibility work. |

> **Bottom line:** *white-filled, dark-halo icons* are the most reliable baseline. Anything else is a visual accent on top of that baseline.

---

## 2. Use **SDF (Signed-Distance-Field) sprites** – they let MapLibre draw a halo/outline at render time.

### 2.1. Build an SDF sprite sheet

1. **Create a single-color SVG** (the “core” shape) – no fill colour, just the path.
2. **Add a 1-pixel transparent padding** around the viewBox (so the halo isn’t clipped).
3. **Export the SVG** and run it through a sprite generator that marks the image as SDF (e.g. `mapbox-gl-sprite` with `--sdf` flag, or the `sprite-packer` script in the MapLibre repo).  
   The resulting PNG will have the distance field encoded in the alpha channel.

```bash
# Example using mapbox-gl-sprite (install globally first)
mapbox-gl-sprite -i icons/ -o sprite@2x.png --sdf
# This also creates sprite.json that maps ids → coordinates
```

### 2.2. Load the sprite at runtime (if you need dynamic icons)

```js
// fetch the SVG, turn it into an Image, then add it as an SDF sprite
async function addSdfIcon(id, svgUrl) {
  const resp = await fetch(svgUrl);
  const svgText = await resp.text();
  const blob = new Blob([svgText], {type: 'image/svg+xml'});
  const img = new Image();
  img.src = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  await img.decode();               // wait for rasterisation
  map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2 });
}

// Example
addSdfIcon('park-marker', '/icons/park.svg');
```

> **Why SDF?** Only SDF images respect the `icon-halo-color` / `icon-halo-width` paint properties. A normal PNG will never get a halo.

---

## 3. Paint the icon – halo, colour, optional inner-badge

```json
{
  "id": "park-points",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "my-points",
  "source-layer": "parks",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "park-marker",          // the SDF id you added
    "icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"], 5, 0.6, 12, 1.2],
    "icon-allow-overlap": true,
    "icon-ignore-placement": false
  },
  "paint": {
    // 1️⃣ White body (the part that will be recoloured by `icon-color`)
    "icon-color": "#ffffff",

    // 2️⃣ Dark semi-transparent halo – works on any background
    "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
    "icon-halo-width": 2,                 // pixels, adjust per zoom if needed
    "icon-halo-blur": 0.5,

    // 3️⃣ Optional inner semantic colour (e.g. green for parks)
    //    Use a second SDF layer on top of the white one, or a separate
    //    “badge” sprite that you position with `icon-offset`.
    //    Example using a second layer:
    //    (see section 4 for the “badge” pattern)
  }
}
```

### Halo width tips

| Zoom range | `icon-halo-width` |
|------------|-------------------|
| 5-8 (small icons) | 1.5-2 |
| 9-12 (medium) | 2-2.5 |
| 13+ (large) | 2.5-3 |

You can also **interpolate** the halo width by zoom if you want a thinner halo on very large icons.

---

## 4. Add a **semantic “badge”** without breaking the halo

If you still need a colour cue (green for parks, amber for historic sites, etc.) keep the **white-halo base** and overlay a tiny coloured shape inside it.

### 4.1. Two-layer approach (recommended)

```json
[
  // ---- Base white icon with dark halo (always drawn first) ----
  {
    "id": "icon-base",
    "type": "symbol",
    "source": "my-points",
    "source-layer": "parks",
    "layout": {
      "icon-image": "park-marker",
      "icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"], 5, 0.6, 12, 1.2],
      "icon-allow-overlap": true
    },
    "paint": {
      "icon-color": "#ffffff",
      "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
      "icon-halo-width": 2,
      "icon-halo-blur": 0.5
    }
  },

  // ---- Small coloured badge placed at the bottom-right of the base ----
  {
    "id": "icon-badge",
    "type": "symbol",
    "source": "my-points",
    "source-layer": "parks",
    "layout": {
      "icon-image": "badge-circle",          // a tiny SDF circle (e.g. 12 px)
      "icon-size": 0.5,
      "icon-allow-overlap": true,
      "icon-offset": [8, -8]                 // shift relative to the base icon
    },
    "paint": {
      "icon-color": "#6abf5c",               // semantic green
      "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
      "icon-halo-width": 1,
      "icon-halo-blur": 0.3
    }
  }
]
```

*Result*: a white-halo icon that never camouflages, plus a tiny coloured dot that still tells the user “this is a park”.

### 4.2. Single-layer “inner-fill” trick (if you want fewer layers)

If you can afford a **two-tone SDF** (white outer shape, coloured inner shape baked into the SVG), you can recolour the inner part with `icon-color` **and** keep the halo. The trick is to set the SVG’s fill to `currentColor` for the outer shape and a second path to `rgba(255,255,255,0)` (transparent) – the inner colour will be supplied by `icon-color`. This is more advanced; the two-layer method is easier to maintain.

---

## 5. Add a **contact-shadow** (ground anchor) – makes the icon feel “placed”

A contact shadow is a **blurred ellipse** directly under the icon, not a drop-shadow that floats.

```json
{
  "id": "icon-shadow",
  "type": "symbol",
  "source": "my-points",
  "source-layer": "parks",
  "layout": {
    "icon-image": "shadow-ellipse",   // a simple SDF ellipse (transparent centre)
    "icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"], 5, 0.6, 12, 1.2],
    "icon-allow-overlap": true,
    "icon-ignore-placement": true,
    "icon-offset": [0, 12]            // push it down so it sits under the base icon
  },
  "paint": {
    "icon-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.35)", // dark, semi-transparent
    "icon-halo-width": 0,
    "icon-opacity": 0.7
  }
}
```

Place this **below** the base icon (i.e. earlier in the `layers` array). The shadow will automatically scale with the icon size because it inherits the same `icon-size` expression.

---

## 6. Layer ordering – keep the **basemap labels on top** of everything

When you inject your custom point layers into an existing satellite style, **insert them before the first symbol layer** (the layer that draws the basemap’s place-name labels). Example:

```js
const style = await map.getStyle();
const firstSymbolId = style.layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Add shadow first (bottom), then base icon, then badge
map.addLayer(shadowLayer, firstSymbolId);
map.addLayer(baseIconLayer, firstSymbolId);
map.addLayer(badgeLayer, firstSymbolId);
```

If you forget this, your icons will sit *above* the basemap labels and obscure them – the opposite of what you want.

---

## 7. Performance & practical tips

| Tip | Reason |
|-----|--------|
| **Keep `icon-allow-overlap: true`** for dense point clouds. | Prevents the engine from trying to hide many icons (which can cause flicker). |
| **Set `icon-ignore-placement: false`** unless you deliberately want icons to hide each other. | Allows the halo to be considered in collision detection, keeping icons from overlapping in a way that would make the halo invisible. |
| **Use `pixelRatio: 2`** when adding the sprite if you target high-DPI screens. | Keeps the halo crisp. |
| **Cache the sprite PNG** (serve with `Cache-Control: max-age=31536000`). | Reduces network round-trips on subsequent map loads. |
| **Test on a few representative tiles** (forest, desert, urban, water). | The halo should be visible on all of them; adjust `icon-halo-width` or `icon-halo-blur` if the halo looks too thick on high-zoom imagery. |
| **Check WCAG contrast** – combine the halo colour with the icon colour (white) and the underlying imagery. The `rgba(0,0,0,0.75)` halo + white text meets the 4.5:1 ratio on virtually any background. | Guarantees accessibility. |

---

## 8. Full example – a ready-to-paste style snippet

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "satellite": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://my.tileserver.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256
    },
    "parks": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "https://my.server.com/parks.geojson"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    { "id": "sat-bg", "type": "raster", "source": "satellite" },

    // ---- Contact shadow (bottom) ----
    {
      "id": "park-shadow",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "parks",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "shadow-ellipse",
        "icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"], 5, 0.6, 12, 1.2],
        "icon-allow-overlap": true,
        "icon-ignore-placement": true,
        "icon-offset": [0, 12]
      },
      "paint": {
        "icon-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.35)",
        "icon-opacity": 0.7,
        "icon-halo-width": 0
      }
    },

    // ---- Base white icon with dark halo ----
    {
      "id": "park-base",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "parks",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "park-marker",
        "icon-size": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["zoom"], 5, 0.6, 12, 1.2],
        "icon-allow-overlap": true
      },
      "paint": {
        "icon-color": "#ffffff",
        "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
        "icon-halo-width": 2,
        "icon-halo-blur": 0.5
      }
    },

    // ---- Small coloured badge (semantic) ----
    {
      "id": "park-badge",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "parks",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "badge-circle",
        "icon-size": 0.5,
        "icon-allow-overlap": true,
        "icon-offset": [8, -8]
      },
      "paint": {
        "icon-color": "#6abf5c",
        "icon-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
        "icon-halo-width": 1,
        "icon-halo-blur": 0.3
      }
    },

    // ---- Basemap label layer (example) ----
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "satellite",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": { /* … */ },
      "paint": { /* … */ }
    }
  ]
}
```

*Replace* `park-marker`, `shadow-ellipse`, and `badge-circle` with the SDF sprites you generated (or load them at runtime with `map.addImage`). The three layers are inserted **before** the first symbol layer (`place-labels`) so they sit under the basemap’s place-name labels.

---

## 9. Quick checklist (copy-paste into your notes)

- [ ] **Generate SDF sprites** (white core, transparent padding).  
- [ ] **Add a dark semi-transparent halo** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) via `icon-halo-*`.  
- [ ] **Set `icon-color` to white** (or very light) for the main body.  
- [ ] **Add a contact-shadow layer** (optional but highly recommended).  
- [ ] **If you need semantic colour**, add a tiny badge layer or a second-tone SDF.  
- [ ] **Insert all custom layers before the first `symbol` layer** of the basemap.  
- [ ] **Test on at least four imagery contexts** (forest, farmland, urban, water).  
- [ ] **Run a contrast check** (white + dark halo ≥ 4.5:1 on typical imagery).  

Follow these steps and your custom point icons will stay **visible, legible, and aesthetically integrated** on any satellite or aerial background. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (or use any existing UI element).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` with a smooth easing curve, a sensible duration, and optional pitch/ bearing changes.  
3. Keep the map’s **visual hierarchy** intact (the fly-to does not affect layer order, but we’ll remind you to keep your data layers under the basemap symbols if you add them later).  

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <!-- MapLibre CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    /* Simple button styling – keep it accessible */
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border: 1px solid #777;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" aria-label="Fly to San Francisco">Fly to SF</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (light-vector basemap)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibre.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // start over San Francisco
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // If you later switch to satellite imagery, remember the halo rules
    // from the cartography skill (white text + dark halo, etc.).
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the target camera position
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const target = {
    // Longitude, latitude (Web Mercator)
    center: [-122.4194, 37.7749], // downtown San Francisco
    zoom: 14,                     // a bit closer
    bearing: 30,                  // rotate the map 30° clockwise
    pitch: 45,                    // tilt the view for a 3-D feel
    // Optional: offset the camera so the point lands off-center
    // offset: [100, -50]   // pixels (right, up)
  };

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Smooth fly-to options
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const flyOptions = {
    // Duration in ms – 1000 ms (1 s) feels smooth for most distances.
    // For very long jumps you can increase it (e.g. 2000 ms) or let
    // MapLibre compute a duration based on distance (`duration: 0`).
    duration: 1200,

    // Easing function – MapLibre ships with a few built-ins.
    // `easeOutCubic` gives a nice “slow-down” feel.
    easing: t => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3),

    // Minimum zoom change before the animation is considered a “jump”.
    // Setting it to 0 forces the animation even for tiny moves.
    minZoom: 0,

    // If you want the camera to stop exactly at the target without
    // overshooting, set `curve` to a low value (default 1.42). 1.0 is
    // a straight line, >1.5 adds a gentle arc.
    curve: 1.2
  };

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Wire the button click → flyTo
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // Combine target + animation options
    map.flyTo({ ...target, ...flyOptions });
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  (Optional) Keep your custom data layers under
  //     the basemap symbols so they don’t hide labels.
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example: add a GeoJSON source and a circle layer.
  // Insert it *before* the first symbol layer.
  map.on('load', () => {
    const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
    map.addSource('my-points', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [{
          type: 'Feature',
          geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.4194, 37.7749] },
          properties: { name: 'SF City Hall' }
        }]
      }
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-points-circle',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'my-points',
      paint: {
        'circle-radius': 6,
        'circle-color': '#ff6600',
        // A thin halo helps the point stay visible on any basemap,
        // especially on satellite imagery.
        'circle-stroke-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.75)',
        'circle-stroke-width': 1.2
      }
    }, firstSymbolId); // <-- insert before symbols
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why this works well (cartographic rationale)

| Aspect | What we did | Why it matters |
|--------|--------------|----------------|
| **Easing & duration** | `easeOutCubic`, `duration: 1200 ms` | Gives a natural “slow-down” feel and avoids a jarring jump. |
| **Pitch & bearing** | `pitch: 45`, `bearing: 30` | Adds depth on imagery or hill-shade basemaps, but keep the pitch ≤ 60° for readability of labels (steeper pitches can make text hard to read). |
| **Halo for point symbols** | `circle-stroke-color` with semi-transparent dark halo | Guarantees the point stays legible on any background, especially satellite imagery where colors vary wildly. |
| **Layer insertion order** | Insert custom layers before the first `symbol` layer | Prevents your data from covering the basemap’s place-name labels (the most common mistake). |
| **Accessibility** | Button has `aria-label`, focus outline, and a minimum font size; text colors follow the “white-on-dark-halo” rule for imagery (if you later switch to satellite basemap). | Meets WCAG contrast requirements and provides keyboard-accessible UI. |

---

#### Quick cheat-sheet for `flyTo` options

| Option | Typical value | Effect |
|--------|---------------|--------|
| `center` | `[lon, lat]` | Destination coordinate |
| `zoom` | `12-16` for city-scale | How close you get |
| `bearing` | `0-360` | Map rotation (clockwise) |
| `pitch` | `0-60` | Tilt; > 60° can make labels unreadable |
| `duration` | `800-1500` ms (or `0` for auto) | Speed of animation |
| `easing` | `t => 1-Math.pow(1-t,3)` (cubic out) | Shape of speed curve |
| `curve` | `1.0-1.5` (default 1.42) | How “arched” the flight path is |
| `offset` | `[x, y]` in pixels | Shift the focal point off-center (useful for UI panels) |

Feel free to tweak any of these values to match the feel of your application. The pattern above works for **any** button, link, or programmatic trigger—just replace `target` with the coordinates you need. Happy mapping!
